proof.text:
Let $U,V\subseteq E$ be disjoint. If $x\notin U\cup V$, their equation is the equation for $b_0$. If $x\in V$, put $S=V\setminus\{x\}$. Then $U,S,\{x\}$ are pairwise disjoint, $U,S\subseteq F$, and direct substitution gives
$$\frac{b(U\cup V)}{b(U)b(V)}
=\frac{b_0(U\cup S)q(U\cup S,\{x\})}{b_0(U)b_0(S)q(S,\{x\})}
=\frac{q(U,S)q(U\cup S,\{x\})}{q(S,\{x\})}
=q(U,S\cup\{x\})=q(U,V).$$
The middle equality uses the induction equation; the next equality is exactly the assumed cocycle identity on $(U,S,\{x\})$, after multiplication by $q(S,\{x\})^{-1}$. These equations remain valid if $U$ or $S$ is empty.

